<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/clinical-trials-and-evidence">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical trials are <strong>medicine's proof machine</strong> — the randomised comparison that
    separates effective treatments from expensive hope. The phase system, randomisation, blinding
    and pre-registration form the pipeline every drug and vaccine passes. Reading a trial
    critically (who was studied, against what, measured how) is a core medical literacy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four phases and the attrition between them" widget="equationStep" config={trialsCfg} />

  <h2>Reading a trial like a professional</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who was studied?</strong> A trial in healthy 25-year-olds may say nothing about your 78-year-old with kidney disease. Endpoints matter: real outcomes (death, stroke) beat surrogate markers (a cholesterol number) — surrogates have famously misled.</li>
    <li><strong>Randomisation and blinding:</strong> what protects against bias. Non-randomised "trials" are the terrain the causal-inference lesson navigates with greater care.</li>
    <li><strong>Statistical honesty:</strong> pre-registered endpoints (no switching after seeing data), intention-to-treat analysis, and absolute vs relative risk ("doubles risk" from 1-in-a-million to 2-in-a-million is different news from 40% to 80%).</li>
    <li><strong>Meta-analysis and hierarchy of evidence:</strong> one trial is a data point; systematic reviews of many trials are the summit — with publication bias (trials that fail go unpublished) as the permanent asterisk (clinicaltrials.gov exists to fix it).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ICH-GCP guidelines; clinicaltrials.gov registry; Ioannidis, "Why Most Published Research Findings Are False" (2005); CONSORT statement.</p>
</TopicPage>
